  "solution": "With $x=a\\\\sin\\\\theta$, $dx=a\\\\cos\\\\theta\\\\,d\\\\theta$ and $\\\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}}=a\\\\cos\\\\theta$ (for $\\\\theta\\\\in[0,\\\\pi/2]$). Limits $0\\\\to 0$, $a\\\\to\\\\pi/2$. Integral $=\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\pi/2}a^{2}\\\\cos^{2}\\\\theta\\\\,d\\\\theta=a^{2}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\pi/2}\\\\frac{1+\\\\cos 2\\\\theta}{2}d\\\\theta=a^{2}[\\\\theta/2+\\\\sin2\\\\theta/4]_{0}^{\\\\pi/2}=\\\\frac{\\\\pi a^{2}}{4}$.",
